## Little Bo-Peep

Little Bo-Peep has lost her sheep,
And can't tell where to find them;

## Winter

Cold and raw the north wind doth blow,
Bleak in the morning early;

You have not.
Hot gray pease, hot, hot, hot;

Hot gray pease, hot.

## Cross Patch

Cross patch, draw the latch,
Sit by the fire and spin;

## The Old Woman Under a Hill

There was an old woman
Lived under a hill;

## Oh, Dear!

Dear, dear! what can the matter be?
Two old women got up in an apple-tree;

## Thirty Days Hath September

Thirty days hath September,
April, June, and November;

## Baby Dolly

Hush, baby, my dolly, I pray you don't cry,
And I'll give you some bread, and some milk by-and-by;

## Bees

A swarm of bees in May
Is worth a load of hay;

## Come Out to Play

Girls and boys, come out to play,
The moon doth shine as bright as day;

## Old Chairs to Mend

If I'd as much money as I could spend,
I never would cry old chairs to mend;

## Robin and Richard

Robin and Richard were two pretty men,
They lay in bed till the clock struck ten;

## The Clever Hen

I had a little hen, the prettiest ever seen,
She washed me the dishes and kept the house clean;

## Two Birds

There were two birds sat on a stone,
Fa, la, la, la, lal, de;

## Leg Over Leg

Leg over leg,
As the dog went to Dover;

## Lucy Locket

Lucy Locket lost her pocket,
Kitty Fisher found it;

## The Flying Pig

Dickory, dickory, dare,
The pig flew up in the air;

## Hush-A-Bye

Hush-a-bye, baby, on the tree top!
When the wind blows the cradle will rock;

## Three Wise Men of Gotham

Three wise men of Gotham
Went to sea in a bowl;

## Pippen Hill

As I was going up Pippen Hill,
Pippen Hill was dirty;

## The Winds

Mister East gave a feast;
Mister North laid the cloth;

Mister West did his best;
Mister South burnt his mouth

Eating cold potato.

Hame till his bonny wee bit laddie;
Clap, clap handies,

My wee, wee ain.

## Elizabeth

Elizabeth, Elspeth, Betsy, and Bess,
They all went together to seek a bird's nest;

## A Needle and Thread

Old Mother Twitchett had but one eye,
And a long tail which she let fly;

## Cushy Cow

Cushy cow, bonny, let down thy milk,
And I will give thee a gown of silk;

## Wee Willie Winkie

Wee Willie Winkie runs through the town,
Upstairs and downstairs, in his nightgown;

## Simple Simon

Simple Simon met a pieman,
Going to the fair;

As three blind mice?

## Five Toes

This little pig went to market;
This little pig stayed at home;

## A Little Man

There was a little man, and he had a little gun,
And his bullets were made of lead, lead, lead;

## Doctor Foster

Doctor Foster went to Glo'ster,
In a shower of rain;

## The Black Hen

Hickety, pickety, my black hen,
She lays eggs for gentlemen;

And a red nose;
The longer she stands

The shorter she grows.

## Curly-Locks

Curly-locks, Curly-locks, wilt thou be mine?
Thou shalt not wash the dishes, nor yet feed the swine;

## Humpty Dumpty

Humpty Dumpty sat on a wall,
Humpty Dumpty had a great fall;

## Master I Have

Master I have, and I am his man,
Gallop a dreary dun;

## Blue Bell Boy

I had a little boy,
And called him Blue Bell;

## Hush-A-Bye

Hush-a-bye, baby,
Daddy is near;

## Nancy Dawson

Nancy Dawson was so fine
She wouldn't get up to serve the swine;

## Jack and Jill

Jack and Jill went up the hill,
To fetch a pail of water;

## Dance to Your Daddie

Dance to your daddie,
My bonnie laddie;

## The Old Woman from France

There came an old woman from France
Who taught grown-up children to dance;

## T'Other Little Tune

I won't be my father's Jack,
I won't be my father's Jill;

## Pancake Day

Great A, little a,
This is pancake day;

## A Plum Pudding

Flour of England, fruit of Spain,
Met together in a shower of rain;

## A Strange Old Woman

There was an old woman, and what do you think?
She lived upon nothing but victuals and drink;

## Little Fred

When little Fred went to bed,
He always said his prayers;

## Doctor Fell

I do not like thee, Doctor Fell;
The reason why I cannot tell;

## Sing a Song of Sixpence

Sing a song of sixpence,
A pocket full of rye;

## Tommy Tittlemouse

Little Tommy Tittlemouse
Lived in a little house;

## The Derby Ram

As I was going to Derby all on a market-day,
I met the finest ram, sir, that ever was fed upon hay;

## The Hobby-Horse

I had a little hobby-horse,
And it was dapple gray;

## Young Lambs to Sell

If I'd as much money as I could tell,
I never would cry young lambs to sell;

## Boy and the Sparrow

A little cock-sparrow sat on a green tree,
And he chirruped, he chirruped, so merry was he;

## Old Woman, Old Woman

There was an old woman tossed in a basket,
Seventeen times as high as the moon;

## Little Jenny Wren

Little Jenny Wren fell sick,
Upon a time;

## The Old Woman and the Pedlar

There was an old woman, as I've heard tell,
She went to market her eggs for to sell;

## Bobby Snooks

Little Bobby Snooks was fond of his books,
And loved by his usher and master;

## Saw a Ship A-sailing

I saw a ship a-sailing,
A-sailing on the sea;

## The Man in the Moon

The Man in the Moon came tumbling down,
And asked the way to Norwich;

## One, He Loves

One, he loves; two, he loves;
Three, he loves, they say;

## The Hart

The hart he loves the high wood,
The hare she loves the hill;

## The Man of Bombay

There was a fat man of Bombay,
Who was smoking one sunshiny day;

## My Maid Mary

My maid Mary she minds the dairy,
While I go a-hoeing and mowing each morn;

## Love Sixpence

I love sixpence, a jolly, jolly sixpence,
I love sixpence as my life;

## One, Two, Buckle My Shoe

One, two,
Buckle my shoe;

Three, four,
Knock at the door;

Five, six,
Pick up sticks;

Seven, eight,
Lay them straight;

Nine, ten,
A good, fat hen;

Eleven, twelve,
Dig and delve;

Thirteen, fourteen,
Maids a-courting;

Fifteen, sixteen,
Maids in the kitchen;

## The Little Girl with a Curl

There was a little girl who had a little curl
Right in the middle of her forehead;

## A Cock and Bull Story

The cock's on the housetop blowing his horn;
The bull's in the barn a-threshing of corn;

## The Kilkenny Cats

There were once two cats of Kilkenny.
Each thought there was one cat too many;

## Old Grimes

Old Grimes is dead, that good old man,
We ne'er shall see him more;

## A Chimney

Black within and red without;
Four corners round about.

## The Man Who Had Naught

There was a man and he had naught,
And robbers came to rob him;

## The Tailors and the Snail

Four and Twenty tailors
Went to kill a snail;

## Around the Green Gravel

Around the green gravel the grass grows green,
And all the pretty maids are plain to be seen;

## Intery, Mintery

Intery, mintery, cutery corn,
Apple seed and apple thorn;

## Hector Protector

Hector Protector was dressed all in green;
Hector Protector was sent to the Queen.

## Rock-A-Bye, Baby

Rock-a-bye, baby, thy cradle is green;
Father's a nobleman, mother's a queen;

## Bessy Bell and Mary Gray

Bessy Bell and Mary Gray,
They were two bonny lasses;

## Dance, Thumbkin Dance

Dance, Thumbkin, dance;
(keep the thumb in motion

## The Little Bird

Once I saw a little bird
Come hop, hop, hop;

## Birds of a Feather

Birds of a feather flock together,
And so will pigs and swine;

## The Old Woman of Leeds

There was an old woman of Leeds,
Who spent all her time in good deeds;

## Jack Jingle

Little Jack Jingle, He used to live single;
But when he got tired of this kind of life,

## The Quarrel

My little old man and I fell out;
I'll tell you what 'twas all about,—

## The Pumpkin-Eater

Peter, Peter, pumpkin-eater,
Had a wife and couldn't keep her;

## Betty Blue

Little Betty Blue
Lost her holiday shoe;

## That's All

There was an old woman sat spinning,
And that's the first beginning;

## My Little Maid

High diddle doubt, my candle's out
My little maid is not at home;

## For Want of a Nail

For want of a nail, the shoe was lost;
For want of the shoe, the horse was lost;

## The Crooked Sixpence

There was a crooked man, and he went a crooked mile,
He found a crooked sixpence beside a crooked stile;

## The Donkey

Donkey, donkey, old and gray,
Ope your mouth and gently bray;

## The King of France

The King of France went up the hill,
With twenty thousand men;

## Peter Piper

Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers;
A peck of pickled peppers Peter Piper picked.

## One to Ten

1, 2, 3, 4, 5!
I caught a hare alive;

## Come, Let's to Bed

"To bed! To bed!"
Says Sleepy-head;

## Multiplication is Vexation

Multiplication is vexation,
Division is as bad;

## Little King Boggen

Little King Boggen, he built a fine hall,
Pie-crust and pastry-crust, that was the wall;

## Taffy

Taffy was a Welshman, Taffy was a thief,
Taffy came to my house and stole a piece of beef;

## The Old Woman of Harrow

There was an old woman of Harrow,
Who visited in a wheelbarrow;

## The Piper and His Cow

There was a piper had a cow,
And he had naught to give her;

## A Thorn

I went to the wood and got it;
I sat me down to look for it

## The Old Woman of Surrey

There was an old woman in Surrey,
Who was morn, noon, and night in a hurry;

## Boy and Girl

There was a little boy and a little girl
Lived in an alley;

## When

When I was a bachelor
I lived by myself;

## London Bridge

London Bridge is broken down,
Dance over my Lady Lee;

## Hot Codlins

There was a little woman, as I've been told,
Who was not very young, nor yet very old;

## Swan

Swan, swan, over the sea;
Swim, swan, swim!

## Christmas

Christmas is coming, the geese are getting fat,
Please to put a penny in an old man's hat;

## The Mouse and the Clock

Hickory, dickory, dock!
The mouse ran up the clock;

## The Woman of Exeter

There dwelt an old woman at Exeter;
When visitors came it sore vexed her,

## Sneezing

If you sneeze on Monday, you sneeze for danger;
Sneeze on a Tuesday, kiss a stranger;

## Pussy-Cat by the Fire

Pussy-cat sits by the fire;
How can she be fair?
